          "id": "sec-1-5",
          "secNumber": "1.5",
          "heading": "Aggregates and packing",
          "content": "## 1.2 Aggregates and packing\n\n### Physical explanation\nAggregate grading controls how many voids must be filled by paste; shape and surface texture also alter water demand and pumpability. A gap in the particle distribution can raise paste demand or susceptibility to segregation even when the nominal maximum size looks acceptable. A combined grading is the mass-weighted passing fraction of each stockpile at every sieve, but a good calculated curve is not enough if stockpiles segregate in storage.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nAn aggregate blend uses 42% sand and 58% coarse aggregate by dry mass. At a 4.75 mm sieve the passing proportions are 97% and 6%. The blended passing fraction is 0.42\u00d797 + 0.58\u00d76 = 44.22%, not the simple arithmetic average. Repeat at all sieves before interpreting a distribution curve.\n\n### Application A: long-span bridge deck\n\nConsider long-span bridge deck, where the central practical constraints include chloride-bearing splash exposure, congested sections, temperature variations and surface curing quality. The lesson on **aggregates and packing** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for low-leakage performance, cracking control, test quality and through-wall penetrations; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: water retaining structure\n\nNow transfer the principle to water retaining structure. The governing constraint changes to low-leakage performance, cracking control, test quality and through-wall penetrations. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **causal investigation** lens: Treat the first explanation as a hypothesis rather than an established diagnosis. Construct a comparison that changes only one controllable variable, records the alternatives and repeats enough times to reveal dispersion. If a test result disagrees with the model, look for changed materials, incorrect basis conversions and boundary-condition mismatch before changing equations.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nWhy could two sources with identical sieve curves produce different slump? How would flat or elongated particles change the conclusion?\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **aggregates and packing**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
