          "id": "sec-1-6",
          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Mixing water and water quality",
          "content": "## 1.3 Mixing water and water quality\n\n### Physical explanation\nWater does two different jobs: it provides a medium for cement reactions and gives a fresh mixture enough mobility to be placed and consolidated. The relevant w/cm ratio uses the mass of total mixing water divided by the mass of all cementitious binders. Excess free water can increase connected porosity after evaporation, while insufficient workable paste can also cause honeycombing. Drinkability is a screening clue, not a blanket substitute for tests when a source contains unfamiliar dissolved compounds.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nA mixture contains 168 kg water, 300 kg portland cement and 70 kg fly ash. The w/cm ratio is 168/(300+70) = 0.454. Reporting 168/300 = 0.56 would confuse water-to-cement with water-to-cementitious ratio; the distinction matters when comparing mixtures with SCMs.\n\n### Application A: mass foundation pour\n\nConsider mass foundation pour, where the central practical constraints include member thickness, restrained cooling, heat release, access for curing and temperature instrumentation. The lesson on **mixing water and water quality** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for repeated wetting, deicing contamination, drainage and repair access; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: elevated parking deck\n\nNow transfer the principle to elevated parking deck. The governing constraint changes to repeated wetting, deicing contamination, drainage and repair access. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **design consequence** lens: Connect the material result to a structural or construction requirement. A difference in a laboratory trend may have no meaningful effect on placing a specific member; conversely, an unmeasured field effect may dominate the nominal design margin. Explicitly identify who is responsible for making a standards-based acceptance determination.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nWhat tests would you order for a new recycled-washwater source? Why can an admixture create good slump at a low w/cm?\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **mixing water and water quality**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
