        {
          "id": "sec-2-9",
          "secNumber": "2.9",
          "heading": "Extended reading: hydration experiments and evidence quality",
          "content": "## Extended reading: hydration experiments and evidence quality\n\n### Reaction progress is not one number\nThe convenient image of cement as a powder that uniformly \u201csets\u201d hides a set of reactions beginning at different rates. Silicate reaction products develop a binding skeleton, while aluminate chemistry and sulfate availability influence early behavior. As hydration progresses, water has to move through an evolving porous network toward unreacted regions. Consequently two cements can reach similar strengths after several weeks through different early pathways. A single degree-of-reaction curve is useful for teaching sensitivity but cannot resolve phase-dependent reactions or substitute for calorimetry, X-ray phase analysis, thermogravimetry and microstructural examination used together.\n\nAn isothermal calorimeter measures a heat flow rate relative to its instrument method; integration gives cumulative heat under particular conditions. The curve often contains periods of low apparent activity, acceleration and subsequent deceleration. Peaks should not be assigned blindly to exact reactions because cement composition, admixtures and supplementary binders modify timing and overlap. For a trial matrix, keep water-to-binder ratio and test temperature controlled while changing one material parameter at a time. Look for the pattern across replicates rather than reporting one peak as a fundamental constant of the mixture. A separate specimen program can examine when changes in calorimetric response are reflected in strength and resistivity.\n\n### Temperature history changes the meaning of age\nA concrete element at 12\u00b0C is not equivalent to an otherwise identical element at 30\u00b0C after the same number of clock hours. Maturity methods summarize aspects of the temperature-time history; the Nurse\u2013Saul formulation accumulates the temperature above a chosen datum. It is convenient for exposing the difference between calendar age and curing temperature, but the resulting index cannot determine strength until a relationship is calibrated for that mixture using appropriate tests. A mixture change invalidates simple carryover of the calibration. Extremely cold temperatures, high early temperatures and changes in reaction pathways also limit a simplistic interpretation of one index.\n\nThe lab's synthetic temperature curve deliberately offers three inputs: mean temperature, daily swing and datum temperature. If the datum is changed, the numerical index changes even though the actual temperature record does not. This is not a failure of mathematics; it reveals that the index depends on a chosen model. Students should plot the same physical temperature history with two proposed datum values, explain the difference, and make clear why a contractor cannot use an arbitrary maturity threshold as a release criterion.\n\n### Heat balance and real temperature gradients\nAn adiabatic estimate divides released energy per cubic metre by volumetric heat capacity. It gives a useful upper-bound scale if the assumed heat is consistent with the period being considered. However, real large members exchange heat with formwork, air, soil and curing water. Temperature gradients, not just the maximum concrete temperature, are critical because the core and surface can expand and cool differently. Thermal strain may be partly free and partly restrained by adjacent material. Early tensile strength and creep are changing simultaneously, so one fixed elastic stress calculation is inadequate for crack prediction.\n\nA lumped model assumes spatial uniformity and balances heating against one heat-loss coefficient. It is intentionally a learning model rather than a solution for a three-dimensional raft foundation. Students should vary binder content and heat-loss factor to identify the direction of the peak-temperature change, then draw a member section with core and surface sensor positions. A separate thermal analysis would need geometry, thermal diffusivity, boundary convection and actual heat-generation kinetics. A concrete temperature sensor should be cross-checked against an ambient record; a mislocated probe can exaggerate or hide a gradient.\n\n### Curing is moisture management, not a calendar ritual\nHydration needs suitable moisture and temperature. Curing practices might include retaining water, limiting evaporation with membranes, wet coverings, or controlling environmental exposure. The chosen method should match the geometry, surface access, temperature and desired performance. Losing moisture early near a surface can have consequences for transport and abrasion even when later cylinder strengths appear reasonable. Moisture supply is especially significant for dense systems that may self-desiccate. A claim such as \u201ccured seven days\u201d is incomplete without evidence of what humidity or temperature the concrete actually experienced.\n\nAn effective comparison of curing methods uses matched mixtures, recorded initial conditions, replicated specimens and at least one measurement sensitive to surface quality. A strength test alone may miss the effect of poor near-surface curing on absorption. The response variable must match the mechanism being investigated. Record when curing began, how continuity was maintained, and any interruption from finishing or early exposure. Those operational details are often more valuable than another digit in the assumed hydration-model rate constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-2-10",
          "secNumber": "2.10",
          "heading": "Integrated case 2 \u2014 Thermal monitoring of an insulated concrete foundation",
          "content": "## Integrated case 2 \u2014 Thermal monitoring of an insulated concrete foundation\n\n**Project setting.** A moderately large foundation is placed under warm daytime conditions, while the forecast predicts a cool night and a windy morning. The mix contains enough binder to generate significant heat during its early reaction. Construction managers want to remove formwork quickly, but the engineering team is concerned about temperature differences between the interior and surface. Their initial model predicts one uniform concrete temperature.\n\n**First estimate.** Consider 390 kg/m\u00b3 binder and an assumed cumulative heat release of 230 kJ/kg over the investigated early period. Released energy is 89.7 MJ/m\u00b3. Dividing by a hypothetical volumetric heat capacity of 2.4 MJ/(m\u00b3\u00b7K) gives a no-loss temperature-rise scale near 37.4 K. This is a useful check on order of magnitude, not a prediction of a real peak. The actual reaction rate changes with temperature and binder composition, and heat moves to soil, formwork and the exposed environment. A concrete section is not a perfectly insulated laboratory calorimeter.\n\n**Measurement strategy.** Place calibrated temperature sensors at several depths from core to surface, adding an ambient sensor outside direct solar heating. Record timestamps and elevations so the thermal profile is reconstructable. Log whether insulation, wet coverings or wind barriers change during curing. A single sensor at an arbitrary point cannot demonstrate that the gradient is controlled. Compare the measured response with a simplified lumped result only to identify the missing physics: internal conduction and boundary transfer. If gradients are important, use a spatial transient heat-conduction analysis supported by material thermal properties and measured boundary conditions.\n\n**Curing and construction choices.** Form removal may increase surface cooling while core temperature remains high. Added insulation may reduce a steep gradient but could increase peak internal temperature if it substantially limits heat dissipation. These are competing effects requiring a project-specific analysis. Any maturity-strength relationship used to plan removal needs calibration for the actual mixture and relevant testing; the arbitrary model settings in a classroom lab are not adequate. Document the basis of the concrete's assumed early tensile properties and restraint conditions when interpreting cracking risk.\n\n**Investigation.** Change binder mass and cooling factor in the heat-model laboratory and note peak timing as well as peak magnitude. Then sketch two plausible cross-section temperature profiles with different surface conditions. Ask which profile might create stronger restrained tension even if it produces a lower mean concrete temperature. A useful final report should provide measured conditions, assumptions, uncertainty and the escalation condition for independent thermal evaluation.\n\n\n### Unit assessment (minimum three substantive questions)\n\n**Question 1 \u2014 evidence:** What evidence from calorimetry, bound-water analysis and thermal measurement would indicate distinct phases of hydration?\n\n**Question 2 \u2014 calculation:** Rework the example under **Water availability, pores and curing** with a 10% change to a plausible physical input, showing the units and documenting whether the conclusion changes. Only change values for which the model or the case supplies a meaningful dependence.\n\n**Question 3 \u2014 decision:** How would a thick raft differ from a thin wall in its temperature profile? Why is peak core temperature not the only cracking concern?\n\n**Question 4 \u2014 comparison:** How can a lab separate genuine densification from a change in microscopy threshold or specimen hydration during preparation?\n\n**Question 5 \u2014 investigative design:** Propose an experiment that can distinguish two explanations of a concrete defect linked to hydration, heat and microstructure. Define specimen/sample selection, controlled variables, measurements, interpretation threshold and expected limitations.\n\n### Unit assessment answer guidance\n\nThe assessment is intentionally open ended: different answers can be defensible if supported by correct physics, clearly identified parameters and realistic controls. Credit requires a reproducible calculation where requested, a reasoned explanation of why a field measurement may differ from a laboratory result, and a test that could falsify the claim. Cite an applicable current local standard when proposing acceptance, and never infer such acceptance from the educational simulations alone."
        }
