          "id": "sec-3-4",
          "secNumber": "3.4",
          "heading": "Workability as a system property",
          "content": "## 3.1 Workability as a system property\n\n### Physical explanation\nWorkability includes the ability to mix, transport, place, consolidate and finish without objectionable segregation. Slump is only one operational indicator and does not uniquely identify viscosity or segregation resistance. A high-range water reducer can increase slump without an equivalent increase in free water, which is why slump alone does not reveal w/cm.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nTwo trial batches each give 170 mm slump. The first has a moderate paste volume and admixture; the second uses additional water and little admixture. Their strength and durability expectations differ even though one observed number matches. The engineer must compare mixture records and appropriate fresh tests.\n\n### Application A: mid-rise residential slab\n\nConsider mid-rise residential slab, where the central practical constraints include restrained slab formwork, finishing timing, transport history and the position of reinforcement. The lesson on **workability as a system property** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for demoulding schedule, moisture retention, batch consistency and accelerated curing; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: precast yard\n\nNow transfer the principle to precast yard. The governing constraint changes to demoulding schedule, moisture retention, batch consistency and accelerated curing. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **measurement** lens: Choose at least two measurements that respond to different mechanisms. State exactly what each test measures, in which units, and whether the test observes a specimen, the full member, or only a local surface. A simple correlation does not establish causation. Report sampling variation separately from variation in the real production process.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nWhat would an increase in slump accompanied by visible bleeding suggest? What would you inspect if the pump pressure becomes unexpectedly high?\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **workability as a system property**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
