        {
          "id": "sec-3-8",
          "secNumber": "3.8",
          "heading": "synthesis and capstone",
          "content": "## Unit 3 synthesis and capstone\n\nTwo mix designs pass the target slump but one produces voids in a heavily reinforced mockup. Plan a repeatable placement test, including mixing time, discharge sequence, vibration regime, air and temperature records. Form hypotheses for blockages, bleeding and segregation separately. Recommend evidence required before either mixture can be accepted for the actual element. Include documentation of worker safety and the distinction between changing admixture dose and adding water.\n\n### Required deliverables\n\n1. A numbered data sheet with observed values and units. 2. A clear calculation trail that identifies assumptions and distinguishes SSD from oven-dry references where relevant. 3. A plotted simulation scenario with at least two parameter changes and a CSV export. 4. A comparison between what the model predicts and what a laboratory test would actually observe. 5. A one-page decision that states limitations, failure consequences and the next evidence collection step.\n\n### Reflection\nThe unit is organized around **fresh concrete and rheology** rather than one prescribed answer. Students should contrast early performance against long-term durability, numerical agreement against experimental validity, and a nominal material property against changes caused by construction. Conclusions are provisional until material, specimen and environmental evidence is available."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-9",
          "secNumber": "3.9",
          "heading": "Extended reading: how fresh properties govern placement decisions",
          "content": "## Extended reading: how fresh properties govern placement decisions\n\n### Workability is not a scalar\nFresh concrete is a suspension of particles in reactive paste. Its behavior depends on stress level, shear history, temperature, elapsed time and the interaction of particles with admixtures. Slump measures a particular deformation under gravity after standardized handling; it does not separately report yield stress, viscosity, segregation resistance, pumpability or passing behavior through reinforcement. A mixture can show a large slump and still segregate. Another can have a moderate slump yet consolidate well with the planned equipment. The appropriate question is whether the concrete can be transported, placed and finished in the required member under the actual site process.\n\nRheological discussions often begin with a Bingham-like approximation: after yield, applied shear stress is an intercept plus plastic viscosity times shear rate. That educational representation allows students to distinguish the onset of flow from resistance during continued deformation. Actual fresh concrete can exhibit thixotropy, structural buildup, shear-dependent behavior and changes during hydration. A fitted Bingham curve might approximate a given observation range without describing the behavior after rest or repeated pumping. Therefore the lab is a mathematical comparison tool, not a numerical translation from rheometer settings to slump or a prediction of formwork pressure.\n\n### Time sequence must be documented\nConsider a truck arriving after a traffic delay. Workability measurements at the plant, arrival and discharge may differ even if no extra water was added. Temperature affects reaction rate and evaporation, prolonged mixing changes air and dispersion, and the timing of admixture addition can change the observed response. For any comparison, the data sheet should include batching timestamp, water additions, temperature, equipment and testing timestamp. Without these records, it is easy to blame a cement source for a slump-loss pattern that actually depends on transport or testing time.\n\nIt is also necessary to distinguish setting from loss of apparent flow. A mixture may lose mobility while the bulk chemical reactions are still developing; changes in flocculation can affect the fresh skeleton without implying that its final set has been reached. Conversely, a delayed set is not necessarily evidence of adequate workability during placement. Process decisions require separate monitoring of those attributes. Any extra water or chemical admixture proposed on site needs to be authorized within the applicable mix specifications and recorded so subsequent strength and durability results can be interpreted.\n\n### Stability and segregation are separate design objectives\nA paste-rich mixture may flow readily yet suffer bleeding or plastic settlement, while one with insufficient fine material may shed coarse particles during transport. Segregation causes spatial changes in composition and can make the surface and interior behave differently. Excessive vibration can drive separation in an unstable mixture; insufficient vibration can leave air pockets and honeycombing. The correct compaction strategy therefore depends on both the material and the element geometry. The laboratory should study how aggregate blend, paste volume, viscosity modifying admixtures and water reducer doses affect stability under representative handling.\n\nBleeding deserves particular attention near slab surfaces and horizontal reinforcement. Migrating water can create weak layers or local channels. Premature finishing may trap surface water beneath a worked surface, leading to delamination or dusting. The presence of a visible surface sheen should be investigated before finishing rather than hidden with additional cement or dry dusting. Site training should include recognizing the condition, recording environmental effects and adjusting the timing of operations in an authorized way.\n\n### Self-consolidating concrete requires multiple checks\nSelf-consolidating systems are designed to fill and pass through obstacles under their own weight while resisting segregation. Flowing ability, passing ability and stability are distinct. A high spread diameter is not sufficient proof that large particles will pass congested reinforcement. A mixture can block even when it looks fluid in an unobstructed test. Relevant trial measurements and full-scale mockups help reveal how aggregate size, bar spacing, viscosity and admixture compatibility interact.\n\nFor a learning trial, select two mixes with equal nominal w/cm but different fine-particle distribution. Record the flow curve and a stability observation after a fixed rest period. Predict which mix may require more vibration and what quality defect would be likely if the wrong procedure were applied. The conclusions should be stated as hypotheses until mockup or field measurements support them. This discipline prevents a single convenient test result from standing in for the entire process."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-3-10",
          "secNumber": "3.10",
          "heading": "Integrated case 3 \u2014 Two concrete mixtures share one slump result",
          "content": "## Integrated case 3 \u2014 Two concrete mixtures share one slump result\n\n**Project setting.** A bridge parapet contains closely spaced reinforcement near several anchor pockets. The subcontractor proposes two concrete mixtures that each show a nominal 180 mm slump shortly after mixing. The site team assumes they are interchangeable. During a trial pour, one produces visible mortar accumulation with coarse particles settling in some regions; the other fills difficult spaces more evenly, though it initially appears slightly harder to push along the form.\n\n**Reasoning from the evidence.** Equal slump does not prove equal stability, viscosity, passing ability or retention. The first material may have inadequate cohesion, a grading imbalance or too much effective water. The second could be appropriately proportioned but may still have poor performance after an extended pumping period. The correct comparison requires timed flow tests, a representative formwork mockup, records of aggregate moisture, air and temperature, and evaluation of segregation. The cause cannot be inferred from visual appearance alone.\n\n**Use of the rheology model.** Consider a simplified Bingham description with a yield stress of 80 Pa and plastic viscosity of 16 Pa\u00b7s. At a shear rate of 5 s\u207b\u00b9, the calculated stress is 80 + 16\u00d75 = 160 Pa. A second hypothetical material with yield stress 50 Pa and viscosity 26 Pa\u00b7s gives 180 Pa at the same rate. Which flows more easily depends on the applied stress and deformation regime; the curves may cross at different rates. The model illustrates that two rheological parameters are needed to describe even this simplified system. It cannot convert those values to a standardized slump or predict a reinforcement-blocking threshold.\n\n**Field trial.** Design a short testing schedule including an initial fresh test, a repeat after expected transport time, observation after passing a congested mockup, and a stability check. Keep mixing history as consistent as possible. Document visible segregation, voids and the amount of intervention needed. Define in advance which defect would lead to rejection or a design revision under the project specification. Avoid correcting a problem by undocumented water addition, because the altered mixture may no longer correspond to tested strength and durability evidence.\n\n**Decision point.** If both mixtures can meet measured strength, their suitability may still differ because of placement. Prefer the material with demonstrated filling and stability under the intended operations rather than the one with the highest free-space flow number. State what additional tests are needed before full production. Students should also identify how a poorly prepared mockup could create an apparent material failure and design a repeat to separate the two explanations.\n\n\n### Unit assessment (minimum three substantive questions)\n\n**Question 1 \u2014 evidence:** What would an increase in slump accompanied by visible bleeding suggest? What would you inspect if the pump pressure becomes unexpectedly high?\n\n**Question 2 \u2014 calculation:** Rework the example under **Segregation and bleeding** with a 10% change to a plausible physical input, showing the units and documenting whether the conclusion changes. Only change values for which the model or the case supplies a meaningful dependence.\n\n**Question 3 \u2014 decision:** Why can a mixture remain highly workable while approaching a later rapid loss of flow? Explain what an instrumented setting-time test actually measures.\n\n**Question 4 \u2014 comparison:** What separate evidence is needed to assess filling ability, passing ability and segregation resistance?\n\n**Question 5 \u2014 investigative design:** Propose an experiment that can distinguish two explanations of a concrete defect linked to fresh concrete and rheology. Define specimen/sample selection, controlled variables, measurements, interpretation threshold and expected limitations.\n\n### Unit assessment answer guidance\n\nThe assessment is intentionally open ended: different answers can be defensible if supported by correct physics, clearly identified parameters and realistic controls. Credit requires a reproducible calculation where requested, a reasoned explanation of why a field measurement may differ from a laboratory result, and a test that could falsify the claim. Cite an applicable current local standard when proposing acceptance, and never infer such acceptance from the educational simulations alone."
        }
