          "id": "sec-4-4",
          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "Absolute-volume balance",
          "content": "## 4.1 Absolute-volume balance\n\n### Physical explanation\nThe absolute-volume method checks whether constituent volumes, including air, sum to the desired concrete yield. Mass is converted to volume using an appropriate density or specific gravity; aggregate values require care because bulk and apparent specific gravities are not interchangeable. The initial quantities are starting estimates subject to testing and correction, not a substitute for trial batching.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nA preliminary mix per cubic metre contains water 170 kg, binder 360 kg with density 3100 kg/m3, sand 800 kg at SSD particle density 2650 kg/m3, coarse aggregate 1020 kg at 2700 kg/m3, and air 2%. The volume sum is 0.170+0.116+0.302+0.378+0.020=0.986 m3, leaving about 0.014 m3 to adjust before claiming one cubic metre yield.\n\n### Application A: precast yard\n\nConsider precast yard, where the central practical constraints include demoulding schedule, moisture retention, batch consistency and accelerated curing. The lesson on **absolute-volume balance** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for restrained slab formwork, finishing timing, transport history and the position of reinforcement; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: mid-rise residential slab\n\nNow transfer the principle to mid-rise residential slab. The governing constraint changes to restrained slab formwork, finishing timing, transport history and the position of reinforcement. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **measurement** lens: Choose at least two measurements that respond to different mechanisms. State exactly what each test measures, in which units, and whether the test observes a specimen, the full member, or only a local surface. A simple correlation does not establish causation. Report sampling variation separately from variation in the real production process.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nWhy would a mistaken aggregate density create a systematic yield error even when all masses are accurately weighed?\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **absolute-volume balance**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
