        {
          "id": "sec-5-8",
          "secNumber": "5.8",
          "heading": "synthesis and capstone",
          "content": "## Unit 5 synthesis and capstone\n\nAn owner has 28-day strengths but needs a decision about deflection and cracking in a long slab. Map compressive testing to modulus, tensile cracking, creep and shrinkage without assuming a universal conversion. Propose specimens and environmental monitoring to estimate the additional properties, identify early-age and drying shrinkage mechanisms, and show how restraint changes stress from free deformation.\n\n### Required deliverables\n\n1. A numbered data sheet with observed values and units. 2. A clear calculation trail that identifies assumptions and distinguishes SSD from oven-dry references where relevant. 3. A plotted simulation scenario with at least two parameter changes and a CSV export. 4. A comparison between what the model predicts and what a laboratory test would actually observe. 5. A one-page decision that states limitations, failure consequences and the next evidence collection step.\n\n### Reflection\nThe unit is organized around **hardened concrete mechanics** rather than one prescribed answer. Students should contrast early performance against long-term durability, numerical agreement against experimental validity, and a nominal material property against changes caused by construction. Conclusions are provisional until material, specimen and environmental evidence is available."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-5-9",
          "secNumber": "5.9",
          "heading": "Extended reading: strength testing and long-term deformation",
          "content": "## Extended reading: strength testing and long-term deformation\n\n### What compression testing actually reports\nA compressive test divides a measured failure load by a prescribed loaded area, but the interpretation rests on specimen geometry, preparation, moisture condition and test procedure. Cylinders, cubes and extracted cores are not automatically interchangeable. End preparation and alignment can affect the stress field in the specimen, while testing equipment and loading rate contribute measurement variation. Field-cured and standard-cured specimens answer different questions. A strength test is consequently a combination of material evidence and measurement-system evidence; a low result should trigger review of both.\n\nStrength develops over time and under different curing histories. The phrase \u201c28-day strength\u201d is incomplete without the specimen type and curing history. Laboratory measurements may be used to establish a mixture's potential, while actual in-place behavior can be influenced by temperature, consolidation and moisture. Acceptance decisions are governed by the applicable specifications, testing methods and relevant jurisdiction; they should not be inferred from the existence of an isolated favorable result. A responsible report lists specimen identifiers, casting and testing times, dimensions, failure loads, calculated stresses and unusual failure descriptions.\n\n### Elastic modulus depends on how it is measured\nA loading curve in concrete is not perfectly linear. A secant modulus relates stress and strain between defined points, while a tangent modulus relates to a local slope. Dynamic tests probe a different response regime. Aggregate stiffness, volume, microcracking and moisture can alter the observed modulus even among concretes with similar compressive strength. It is therefore not defensible to convert one strength measurement into a precision deflection prediction through a universal formula when a project demands reliable deformation estimates.\n\nAn elastic approximation still teaches useful mechanics. Under a stated stress and secant modulus, predicted strain is stress divided by modulus. Students should keep MPa and GPa consistent, then use a strain gauge or displacement measurement to see where the linear idealization ceases to fit. As loading grows, microcrack development can curve the stress\u2013strain relation before a visible macrocrack appears. Observing that curvature is not a mathematical nuisance; it is part of the material response that a designer must understand.\n\n### Shrinkage and creep are different processes\nShrinkage can occur without external mechanical loading, while creep is time-dependent deformation under sustained stress. Drying and autogenous shrinkage have different moisture and reaction drivers. A thick member and a thin member cast from the same concrete may experience different internal moisture gradients, so their strains and cracking patterns may differ. Age at loading also matters because the matrix becomes stiffer and its creep potential evolves as hydration proceeds.\n\nA simplistic multiplication of free shrinkage strain by modulus and restraint can produce a large estimated stress. Real members relax some stress through creep, cracking, reinforcement action and movement of supporting elements. The degree of restraint is spatially variable. A model for restrained shrinkage should ideally include environmental history, member dimensions, stiffness evolution and an appropriate relaxation relationship. The educational stress envelope deliberately applies a fixed relaxation factor to expose sensitivity, but this assumption must never be treated as a design value.\n\n### Crack control is not the same as high strength\nA stronger concrete may possess a higher modulus and suffer greater thermal or autogenous strain under some circumstances. If the mixture cracks early, its later transport performance can be compromised regardless of its high measured compressive strength. This does not mean high strength is undesirable; it means compressive strength must be evaluated together with tensile resistance, fracture behavior, shrinkage, restraint and detailing. The best mixture for an unrestrained compression cylinder may not be the best mixture for a massive, restrained structural element.\n\nTo investigate a serviceability concern, design a specimen program that separately measures strength, modulus, shrinkage and creep under controlled humidity and temperature. Compare field observations of cracks with construction timing and reinforcement layout. In the final report state which mechanism is supported by the evidence and which remains speculative. An unsupported diagnosis can lead to costly repairs that fail to treat the root cause."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-5-10",
          "secNumber": "5.10",
          "heading": "Integrated case 5 \u2014 A high-strength slab cracks early",
          "content": "## Integrated case 5 \u2014 A high-strength slab cracks early\n\n**Project setting.** Two concretes are proposed for a heavily restrained floor. The high-strength candidate has a lower nominal w/cm and a higher reported cylinder strength. A trial panel, however, shows fine cracking shortly after early exposure, while the lower-strength candidate remains visually intact at that time. The project team asks whether the cracking can be ignored because the higher-strength mix exceeds the compressive requirement.\n\n**Mechanisms to test.** First establish when cracks appeared relative to setting, finishing, restraint and curing. Plastic shrinkage is possible before adequate strength development if evaporation outruns available surface water. Thermal contraction can create restraint after an early temperature peak. Drying and autogenous shrinkage may develop at different rates and with different internal moisture histories. A high compressive strength is not a proof of low shrinkage, adequate tensile performance or absence of cracking. Geometry, reinforcement and surface conditions must be included.\n\n**Stress sensitivity.** For a deliberately simplistic calculation, consider 350 microstrain free shrinkage, elastic modulus 30 GPa and effective restraint fraction 0.55. Multiplication without relaxation would give 30,000 MPa\u00d70.000350\u00d70.55 \u2248 5.78 MPa nominal stress. Applying a teaching relaxation fraction of 45% reduces it to about 3.18 MPa. Neither value is a defensible direct predictor of actual crack width. Concrete stiffness, creep, tensile capacity, gradients and cracking change with time; the assumed effective restraint is itself difficult to establish.\n\n**Evidence program.** Prepare matched specimens for compressive strength, modulus, shrinkage under controlled humidity, and any appropriate restrained-cracking test. Measure concrete temperature during the early period and log curing start and interruptions. Affected panels should be mapped for crack width, orientation, position and timing. Compare environmental data across panels rather than assuming the difference lies only in binder content. Where deflection matters, include sustained-load deformation evidence rather than relying solely on elastic modulus inferred from strength.\n\n**Practical conclusion.** The preferred concrete is the one that meets all the required service conditions with acceptable constructability, not necessarily the one with the largest cylinder-strength number. If the high-strength mixture can be modified to control early cracking while retaining the advantages, retest the altered mixture. If not, the alternative may be more reliable. Students should state separately what the observations establish, which mechanisms remain hypotheses and which test outcome would lead them to change their recommendation.\n\n\n### Unit assessment (minimum three substantive questions)\n\n**Question 1 \u2014 evidence:** How might platen preparation, cylinder eccentricity and curing alter the distribution of measured results?\n\n**Question 2 \u2014 calculation:** Rework the example under **Modulus, tensile strength and fracture** with a 10% change to a plausible physical input, showing the units and documenting whether the conclusion changes. Only change values for which the model or the case supplies a meaningful dependence.\n\n**Question 3 \u2014 decision:** How would you distinguish early plastic shrinkage from drying shrinkage that develops over weeks?\n\n**Question 4 \u2014 comparison:** Which additional test schedule and environmental records would make a long-term prediction more defensible?\n\n**Question 5 \u2014 investigative design:** Propose an experiment that can distinguish two explanations of a concrete defect linked to hardened concrete mechanics. Define specimen/sample selection, controlled variables, measurements, interpretation threshold and expected limitations.\n\n### Unit assessment answer guidance\n\nThe assessment is intentionally open ended: different answers can be defensible if supported by correct physics, clearly identified parameters and realistic controls. Credit requires a reproducible calculation where requested, a reasoned explanation of why a field measurement may differ from a laboratory result, and a test that could falsify the claim. Cite an applicable current local standard when proposing acceptance, and never infer such acceptance from the educational simulations alone."
        }
