          "id": "sec-6-5",
          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "Carbonation and corrosion risk",
          "content": "## 6.2 Carbonation and corrosion risk\n\n### Physical explanation\nAtmospheric carbon dioxide reacts with alkaline constituents and can reduce pore-solution alkalinity near the surface. A square-root-of-time depth approximation is common for illustrating diffusion-influenced fronts, but the coefficient depends on exposure, moisture, binder and curing. Reaching reinforcement depth indicates a change in corrosion conditions, not automatic proof that corrosion has occurred.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nA conceptual front model x=k\u221at with k=3 mm/\u221ayear gives x=12 mm after 16 years. If nominal cover is 30 mm, the simple front has not reached steel; this result must not be used to assign a design service life without exposure-dependent calibration and cover variation.\n\n### Application A: water retaining structure\n\nConsider water retaining structure, where the central practical constraints include low-leakage performance, cracking control, test quality and through-wall penetrations. The lesson on **carbonation and corrosion risk** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for chloride-bearing splash exposure, congested sections, temperature variations and surface curing quality; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: long-span bridge deck\n\nNow transfer the principle to long-span bridge deck. The governing constraint changes to chloride-bearing splash exposure, congested sections, temperature variations and surface curing quality. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **causal investigation** lens: Treat the first explanation as a hypothesis rather than an established diagnosis. Construct a comparison that changes only one controllable variable, records the alternatives and repeats enough times to reveal dispersion. If a test result disagrees with the model, look for changed materials, incorrect basis conversions and boundary-condition mismatch before changing equations.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nWhy can a constantly saturated surface carbonate more slowly than a moderately humid one?\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **carbonation and corrosion risk**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
