          "id": "sec-6-6",
          "secNumber": "6.6",
          "heading": "Chloride ingress and reinforcement",
          "content": "## 6.3 Chloride ingress and reinforcement\n\n### Physical explanation\nA diffusion-type model can describe chloride profiles in a saturated idealization, but binding, wetting cycles, cracks and changing surface concentrations complicate reality. Threshold chloride contents vary and are not a single universal number. Corrosion risk should be evaluated with cover, exposure, mix history and actual chloride measurements.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nFor a semi-infinite Fickian illustration, C(x,t)=Cs\u00b7erfc[x/(2\u221a(Dt))]. Doubling cover does not simply double the time to the same concentration; at fixed Cs and D, corresponding penetration times scale approximately with cover squared. The model assumes constant D and surface concentration.\n\n### Application A: elevated parking deck\n\nConsider elevated parking deck, where the central practical constraints include repeated wetting, deicing contamination, drainage and repair access. The lesson on **chloride ingress and reinforcement** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for member thickness, restrained cooling, heat release, access for curing and temperature instrumentation; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: mass foundation pour\n\nNow transfer the principle to mass foundation pour. The governing constraint changes to member thickness, restrained cooling, heat release, access for curing and temperature instrumentation. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **design consequence** lens: Connect the material result to a structural or construction requirement. A difference in a laboratory trend may have no meaningful effect on placing a specific member; conversely, an unmeasured field effect may dominate the nominal design margin. Explicitly identify who is responsible for making a standards-based acceptance determination.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nHow would tidal wetting make a constant-surface-concentration model questionable?\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **chloride ingress and reinforcement**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
