          "id": "sec-6-7",
          "secNumber": "6.7",
          "heading": "Chemical attack, freezing and sulfate action",
          "content": "## 6.4 Chemical attack, freezing and sulfate action\n\n### Physical explanation\nDifferent exposures damage concrete through different paths. Freezing damage depends on saturation and the presence of an appropriate entrained-air void system. Sulfate exposure involves reactions and transport, with binder composition and permeability both relevant. Acidic environments can dissolve hydration products even when compressive strength was initially high.\n\n### Worked engineering interpretation\nAn unair-entrained specimen survives one low-temperature event while an adequately air-entrained specimen withstands many cycles. The comparison does not show that air entrainment makes concrete stronger in compression; entrained air often trades some strength for better freeze\u2013thaw resistance when properly proportioned.\n\n### Application A: pumped high-rise column\n\nConsider pumped high-rise column, where the central practical constraints include long pumping line, workability retention, reinforcement congestion and high lift pressure. The lesson on **chemical attack, freezing and sulfate action** becomes a decision only after a responsible engineer states the exposure, intended function, material evidence and tolerable uncertainty. For this case start by identifying which quantities come from a mixture record and which depend on field construction. Make a table with the current state, intended change and predicted consequence. Do not equate a clean theoretical trend with successful placement: operation-specific limits can reverse the preferred option.\n\nA useful thought experiment is to compare an apparently favorable laboratory value against an observed field defect. If the laboratory result supports the material but the member shows insufficient consolidation or cracking, the next investigation is not to discard the observation. Look for flatness, surface abrasion, bleeding, joint detailing and delayed finishing; these can make the same nominal composition behave differently. State a falsifiable prediction for each alternative. For example, if the issue arises from a changed moisture condition, repeated batch-level corrections should reduce the discrepancy while other variables remain controlled.\n\n### Application B: industrial floor\n\nNow transfer the principle to industrial floor. The governing constraint changes to flatness, surface abrasion, bleeding, joint detailing and delayed finishing. A robust interpretation should revisit the boundary condition, not simply reuse a recommendation developed for the previous setting. Define the two most consequential failure modes and show what evidence would detect them early. The environmental and measurement variables must be recorded before ranking alternatives, particularly when transport, curing or local geometry can differ across a single structure.\n\nFor the **uncertainty** lens: Record which numbers are observed, inferred, assumed or fitted. Vary the two most uncertain inputs over a plausible investigated range rather than claiming that a single prediction is precise. Explain whether a decision remains the same if the model is wrong by 10\u201320%, and describe the additional measurement with highest decision value.\n\n### What the example establishes \u2014 and what it does not\n\nThe calculation or reasoning in this lesson establishes a relationship under explicitly stated inputs. It does not establish that a delivered batch meets contractual limits, that a structural component is safe, or that a laboratory specimen is representative of a field member. To make a stronger claim, identify the applicable testing method, calibration, sampling strategy and decision criterion. Always distinguish a material-property trend from its practical magnitude and the cost of correcting it if the original assumption proves wrong.\n\n### Check your understanding\nList the exposure conditions you must establish before choosing a material durability test.\n\n1. Describe a measurement that would directly challenge the worked scenario.\n2. Explain a realistic competing cause for an unexpected result.\n3. State the units and reference mass or volume basis for every numerical quantity in the lesson.\n4. Propose one low-cost trial that could disprove the preferred explanation before full-scale construction.\n\n### Response guide\nA defensible response should define the mechanism in **chemical attack, freezing and sulfate action**, use the worked example as a transparent test of dimensional and physical consistency, and explain what further observation would change the conclusion. Merely restating a formula without discussing its assumptions is insufficient. A complete answer reports source measurements, explicit uncertainty, and an actionable test plan."
