        {
          "id": "sec-6-8",
          "secNumber": "6.8",
          "heading": "synthesis and capstone",
          "content": "## Unit 6 synthesis and capstone\n\nA coastal stair tower will experience spray and intermittent wetting. Compare two concretes with equal target strength but different binder systems. Develop an exposure map and identify where chloride transport, carbonation, cracking and workmanship may dominate. Use illustrative profiles to define measurements for trial mixes, but do not compute service life without calibrated input and suitable acceptance framework.\n\n### Required deliverables\n\n1. A numbered data sheet with observed values and units. 2. A clear calculation trail that identifies assumptions and distinguishes SSD from oven-dry references where relevant. 3. A plotted simulation scenario with at least two parameter changes and a CSV export. 4. A comparison between what the model predicts and what a laboratory test would actually observe. 5. A one-page decision that states limitations, failure consequences and the next evidence collection step.\n\n### Reflection\nThe unit is organized around **durability and environmental transport** rather than one prescribed answer. Students should contrast early performance against long-term durability, numerical agreement against experimental validity, and a nominal material property against changes caused by construction. Conclusions are provisional until material, specimen and environmental evidence is available."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-6-9",
          "secNumber": "6.9",
          "heading": "Extended reading: transport, exposure and durability inference",
          "content": "## Extended reading: transport, exposure and durability inference\n\n### A low-permeability paste is not automatically a durable member\nConcrete durability depends on the interaction of its material and environment. A dense uncracked matrix may restrict fluid ingress, but a through-crack can provide a much faster local pathway. Surface curing, consolidation and finish quality can create a near-surface zone different from the bulk tested in a laboratory. Transport is further affected by whether the pores are saturated, partially wet or repeatedly cycled. An engineer must therefore specify the expected exposure and the mechanism likely to govern deterioration before selecting laboratory indicators.\n\nSorptivity describes capillary uptake in suitable early-time conditions. Permeability reflects flow through a porous medium under pressure, while diffusion addresses movement driven by concentration gradients. Electrical resistivity can provide indirect information related to pore solution and connectivity, but it is not an interchangeable measurement of every transport process. Two specimens may rank differently across tests because their moisture states, ionic concentrations and pore geometries differ. Where a result is used as a proxy for long-term behavior, the correlation should be demonstrated for the relevant materials and exposure.\n\n### Carbonation is a moving chemical boundary\nCarbon dioxide reacts with alkaline concrete constituents, shifting pore-solution chemistry near the exposed surface. A simple model often illustrates carbonation depth as a coefficient times the square root of exposure time. That relationship captures a possible trend in diffusion-influenced conditions but compresses many mechanisms into one coefficient. Humidity affects both gas movement and chemical reactions; very dry or constantly saturated surfaces can behave differently from moderately moist ones. Binder composition, curing and cracks further change the result.\n\nIf a nominal front reaches reinforcement depth, the concrete near steel may no longer provide the same chemical environment. Corrosion still depends on electrochemical conditions such as oxygen, moisture and local chemistry. Cover is itself variable, and one reported average cover should not be treated as a uniform value. For a sound interpretation, measure or estimate a cover distribution, investigate exposure history, and compare an appropriate profile with multiple cores. The model's calculated \u201cintersection time\u201d is not a warranty of service life.\n\n### Chloride diffusion is an idealization with important assumptions\nA common classroom solution uses the complementary error function for a semi-infinite domain with a constant surface concentration and diffusivity. It is valuable for exploring sensitivity to depth, time and diffusion coefficient. It is less reliable when chloride binding, wetting cycles, aging diffusivity or cracks dominate. A nominal threshold for corrosion initiation is not universal. The actual state depends on steel, pore solution and the exposure history, and the threshold itself may need to be treated as uncertain rather than exact.\n\nA useful laboratory assignment compares the idealized curve with measurements from a series of depth-resolved samples. Students should avoid fitting a high-precision diffusivity to only two noisy points. Instead, define surface concentration assumptions, use multiple depths, report the fit quality, and consider whether the parameter varies with age. When a surface concentration changes seasonally, the simple solution's boundary condition is wrong; adding more decimal places to the fit will not correct the physics.\n\n### Distinguish sulfate, acid and freezing mechanisms\nSulfate-related deterioration involves transport and potentially expansive reactions whose details depend on binder chemistry and environment. Acid attack can dissolve calcium-bearing reaction products. Freeze\u2013thaw damage involves the behavior of pore water during repeated temperature changes, saturation and an appropriate air-void structure. A concrete optimized only for strength may lack the air void system needed for severe freeze\u2013thaw exposure, while increased entrained air may reduce nominal compressive strength. Those tradeoffs demand mechanism-specific tests rather than one generic \u201cdurability index.\u201d\n\nFor an exposed reinforced member, draw a degradation map locating cracks, joints, drainage paths, corners and areas with different cover. Then rank failure mechanisms by evidence and uncertainty. Specify which tests would distinguish carbonation, chloride, freeze\u2013thaw, sulfate or workmanship defects. A repair strategy should address the actual ingress route and exposure, not just the most visible surface symptom."
        },
        {
          "id": "sec-6-10",
          "secNumber": "6.10",
          "heading": "Integrated case 6 \u2014 Chloride exposure in a car park edge beam",
          "content": "## Integrated case 6 \u2014 Chloride exposure in a car park edge beam\n\n**Project setting.** A reinforced edge beam is repeatedly exposed to wind-driven rain and salt-bearing splashes. Engineers have a concrete strength certificate but limited information about cover distribution, surface cracks and chloride transport. One consultant proposes estimating the year of corrosion initiation from a single laboratory diffusion coefficient. Another argues that no useful calculation is possible without decades of measurements.\n\n**Reasonable middle approach.** An idealized diffusion model can identify sensitivity and help plan measurements, but its assumptions must be explicit. Suppose the surface chloride concentration is fixed at 0.8% by binder mass, effective D is 22 mm\u00b2/year and exposure duration is twenty years. With depth 35 mm, the error-function solution gives a concentration of roughly 0.19% binder mass at that location. This result is conditional on a constant boundary concentration, constant diffusivity, a semi-infinite uniform body and no cracks or binding change. It is not a universal steel-corrosion threshold calculation.\n\n**Field evidence.** Measure cover distribution rather than a single nominal cover. Locate visible cracks, joints, drainage paths and repairs. If possible, obtain several depth-resolved chloride profiles in representative exposure zones; record whether surfaces were sampled near a crack or sound concrete. Investigate moisture and exposure history, because repeated wetting changes boundary conditions. If measured profiles depart from the idealized curve, do not silently force the equation to match through an unrealistic fitted D. Evaluate whether the model class is appropriate at all.\n\n**Parallel carbonation concern.** The structure may also experience carbonation, which depends on humidity and the alkalinity of the concrete. A square-root-of-time front model can compare an approximate penetration depth with cover, but that front is not the same as electrochemical corrosion. A dry cover layer could carbonate while corrosion remains moisture-limited; a wet chloride-exposed area can behave differently. Analyze the mechanisms separately and then consider their interaction, rather than using whichever simple equation predicts the shortest remaining life.\n\n**Decision.** Develop a risk-based inspection and testing plan, classify evidence quality and define what observation would trigger repair assessment. Include service consequences and access requirements. The educational simulations offer transparent sensitivity analysis but cannot set a design life or authorize repairs without engineering judgment, verified tests and the applicable standards. The practical distinction is between refusing any model and using a limited model responsibly: the latter is often appropriate when uncertainty is disclosed and the next measurement is chosen to reduce it.\n\n\n### Unit assessment (minimum three substantive questions)\n\n**Question 1 \u2014 evidence:** Which material test would be most informative for a member exposed to repeated wetting rather than permanent submersion?\n\n**Question 2 \u2014 calculation:** Rework the example under **Carbonation and corrosion risk** with a 10% change to a plausible physical input, showing the units and documenting whether the conclusion changes. Only change values for which the model or the case supplies a meaningful dependence.\n\n**Question 3 \u2014 decision:** How would tidal wetting make a constant-surface-concentration model questionable?\n\n**Question 4 \u2014 comparison:** List the exposure conditions you must establish before choosing a material durability test.\n\n**Question 5 \u2014 investigative design:** Propose an experiment that can distinguish two explanations of a concrete defect linked to durability and environmental transport. Define specimen/sample selection, controlled variables, measurements, interpretation threshold and expected limitations.\n\n### Unit assessment answer guidance\n\nThe assessment is intentionally open ended: different answers can be defensible if supported by correct physics, clearly identified parameters and realistic controls. Credit requires a reproducible calculation where requested, a reasoned explanation of why a field measurement may differ from a laboratory result, and a test that could falsify the claim. Cite an applicable current local standard when proposing acceptance, and never infer such acceptance from the educational simulations alone."
        }
